WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:01.210
All right.

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:04.770
So yesterday was day one of the Democratic
National Convention.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.220
I did a livestream of the entire thing.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.460
I was commenting.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.580
I was reading viewer comments as it was all
going on.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.750
And I don't know how else to start other than
to say it really wasn't very good.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:24.859
Now, I'll talk about what expectations are
and how it compares to expectations and to

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:26.859
what we would expect from Trump.

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:28.809
And we're going to contextualize it.

00:00:28.809 --> 00:00:33.800
But there's no way that I could come to you
today and say I'm so inspired, inspired and

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.770
invigorated and energized by it, by what we
saw yesterday.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:45.550
I sent some early tweets, like during the
first half hour, which were really quite negative.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:51.380
I said, first off, quote, I could have done
without the prayer to Jesus to kick off the

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:52.380
DNC.

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.910
Not sure about anybody else.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:57.070
And I get that everybody does it in Joe Biden's
Catholic.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.230
And there's still a pretense about religion
in the Democratic Party.

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.699
And it's not just necessarily a pretense.

00:01:03.699 --> 00:01:04.909
There are religious people as well.

00:01:04.909 --> 00:01:06.460
And that that's fine.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:08.659
They do the Pledge of Allegiance.

00:01:08.659 --> 00:01:12.430
Like, it all just smelled at this point.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:18.680
Like Republican lite, which maybe is the goal
and arguably is still a big part of the these

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.740
centrist elements of the Democratic Party.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.560
So I'm not acting surprised by that.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:29.159
I'm just saying it didn't really resonate
with me then sort of about 24, 22 minutes

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:30.159
in.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:33.439
In fact, I said, someone, please talk me off
the ledge.

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:34.560
I want to win.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.380
And the first 22 minutes of this DNC, I'm
scared.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.820
Can someone tell me how great this is or something?

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.759
But, you know, then it did start to improve.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:48.259
And maybe my view is skewed by the fact that
I just hate cliche packaged politics.

00:01:48.259 --> 00:01:53.630
And that's exactly what the DNC, the Democratic
National Convention is designed to be.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:59.710
It's designed to be a package culminating
with the nomination of, in this case, Joe

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:06.390
Biden in 2016, 26, 2012, different people
to get to the election.

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:10.310
And it's a package, and especially when you've
got coronavirus.

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:16.330
So it's not just the idea of packaging up
a candidate and a platform, but it is quite

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:21.800
literally a video package that has been reproduced
in combination with live segments.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.800
You have no crowd energy.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.700
It might be just as bad with the Republican
National Convention, and this may be just

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:36.160
what it is, but it felt very low energy and,
you know, they'd have an actual okay speech

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.110
and then they'd cut to music or some other
kind of interlude that didn't make sense and

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.160
took energy away.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.370
But I want to try to really see this as not
my reaction.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.370
You know, maybe this is the right thing.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.940
Like, they don't need to convince me.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:56.080
I'm already voting for Joe Biden.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.480
And I actually don't even understand being
undecided at this point.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:10.330
And so maybe this is about a very sort of
polished, inoffensive package meant to appeal

00:03:10.330 --> 00:03:14.920
to the few people that might actually be unsure
about who to vote for.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:17.370
Like I don't know is the point.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:22.740
But given the opportunity for Democrats to
do something, knowing Republicans will also

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:25.910
have an opportunity to do the same thing next
week.

00:03:25.910 --> 00:03:30.040
I'd rather have the better convention than
the worst one, no matter how much it really

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.880
matters in the end.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:36.560
Now, it did keep improving during the first
two hours, during the two hours of the first

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:37.560
day.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:43.150
I thought the best parts of the evening were
a woman named Kristen or Chiesa, whose father

00:03:43.150 --> 00:03:47.070
died of Corona virus after voting for Donald
Trump.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:52.120
She spoke really well and authentically, and
a lot of the stuff felt inauthentic.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.930
She was really good.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:55.700
And then from the big names.

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.790
Listen, Bernie was excellent.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:02.380
Bernie is always just so clear and precise
with his message and the stakes.

00:04:02.380 --> 00:04:03.630
And I liked that.

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:11.350
Bernie spoke specifically to his supporters
from both 2016 and 2020 and talked about the

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:17.930
importance of voting for Joe Biden this time
around because of the existential threat that

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:18.969
is Donald Trump.

00:04:18.969 --> 00:04:20.959
Let's look at like a minute of that.

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:27.650
My friends, I say to you, to everyone who
supported all the candidates in the primary

00:04:27.650 --> 00:04:33.419
and to those who may have voted for Donald
Trump in the last election, the future of

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:36.250
our democracy is at stake.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:39.620
The future of our economy is at stake.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.389
The future of our planet is at stake.

00:04:43.389 --> 00:04:51.180
We must come together, defeat Donald Trump
and elect Joe Biden and Kamala Harris as our

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:54.789
next president and vice president.

00:04:54.789 --> 00:05:08.110
My friends, the price of failure is just too
great to imagine.

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.520
So really nice job by Bernie Sanders.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:14.909
Beautiful esthetics, by the way, also, if
you were watching, Bernie came to us from

00:05:14.909 --> 00:05:17.729
Burlington, Vermont, with the nice wood pile
in the background.

00:05:17.729 --> 00:05:22.069
I mean, just very, very esthetically pleasing
as well.

00:05:22.069 --> 00:05:23.069
I'm half kidding.

00:05:23.069 --> 00:05:24.069
I mean, it was cool.

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:25.090
I don't actually care about that.

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:27.099
But seeing the wood pile was nice.

00:05:27.099 --> 00:05:30.180
And then Michelle Michelle Obama was was really
very good.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.470
She closed off the night.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:35.870
I thought maybe she went a bit too long, which
I was fine with.

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:39.599
But I could see from people in the chat that
some were starting to sort of tune out a little

00:05:39.599 --> 00:05:40.599
bit.

00:05:40.599 --> 00:05:42.999
But I thought she was excellent, fantastic
speaker.

00:05:42.999 --> 00:05:47.919
And again, check out this clip from Michelle
Obama going into this idea of does my vote

00:05:47.919 --> 00:05:49.439
even matter?

00:05:49.439 --> 00:05:52.780
Michelle Obama making the case very strongly
that, yes, it does.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:58.289
I've said before, being president doesn't
change who you are.

00:05:58.289 --> 00:06:00.800
It reveals who you are.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:08.539
Well, a presidential election can reveal who
we are two and four years ago.

00:06:08.539 --> 00:06:13.250
Too many people chose to believe that their
votes didn't matter.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:15.130
Maybe they were fed up.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.509
Maybe they thought the outcome wouldn't be
close.

00:06:17.509 --> 00:06:19.430
Maybe the barriers felt too steep.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.300
Whatever the reason.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:28.360
In the end, those choices sent someone to
the Oval Office who lost the national popular

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:35.720
vote by nearly three million votes in one
of the states that determine the outcome.

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:43.759
The winning margin averaged out to just two
votes per precinct, two votes.

00:06:43.759 --> 00:06:46.400
She took that head on really, really well.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.180
So I think maybe what I needed was like an
attitudinal adjustment.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:53.539
You know, how should we be thinking about
this?

00:06:53.539 --> 00:06:54.539
Does it matter?

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:57.840
As I've said before, I don't need conventions
this year.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.809
I don't need debates this year.

00:06:59.809 --> 00:07:05.289
But if we are having them, I would rather
Joe Biden have the better convention convention

00:07:05.289 --> 00:07:07.120
rather than the worse one.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:08.800
And we'll see how that continues tonight.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.400
Now, Donald Trump next week is planning to
do his at the White House.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:19.340
It'll have apparently more of a live feel
than the very pre produced feel that the DNC

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.319
had, at least on day one.

00:07:21.319 --> 00:07:26.219
My guess is Trump is going to put together
some kind of in-person crowd.

00:07:26.219 --> 00:07:30.289
That would be my my guests now with Corona
virus.

00:07:30.289 --> 00:07:31.289
You've got to be safe.

00:07:31.289 --> 00:07:32.569
You can't do a big thing.

00:07:32.569 --> 00:07:35.809
Packing an arena with 20000 people would be
crazy.

00:07:35.809 --> 00:07:44.180
My idea for the speeches for Bernie and Michelle
and Biden, etc., for those speeches, I would

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:49.000
have instead of doing like 30 short segments,
as they did yesterday, I would have given

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:54.669
the keynote people a little more time, but
made it higher energy by putting together

00:07:54.669 --> 00:07:59.990
some small, safe mass, squaring socially distant
crowds outdoors.

00:07:59.990 --> 00:08:01.130
I'm not saying bring.

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:03.860
I'm not saying do anything unsafe.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:08.990
But I think we could have gotten more energy
without increasing the risk of corona virus

00:08:08.990 --> 00:08:12.110
with some small outdoor crowds.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:13.669
Bernie's crowd in Burlington.

00:08:13.669 --> 00:08:15.360
That's fine.

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:20.909
Michelle's in D.C. or were, you know, wherever
they're living or at this point showing the

00:08:20.909 --> 00:08:23.879
importance of mask wearing, showing the importance
of space.

00:08:23.879 --> 00:08:28.660
But it would have brought a little something
Magennis say, quar that was definitely missing

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:29.660
yesterday.

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:31.039
That would have been my preference.

00:08:31.039 --> 00:08:32.039
Nothing unsafe.

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:34.120
We will see how night to goes.

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:39.620
And I will be streaming starting about 15
minutes before the start of day two tonight

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.530
on YouTube, Twitch and Facebook.

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:43.320
And I hope that you will join me there

